      },
      "lineNbr": 87,
      "author": {
        "id": 1308749
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-07-10T21:15:17Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This macro, and `LED_COLOR_INIT` require that the children nodes are named \"led-0\" and \"led-1\".  This is fragile, as this naming isn\u0027t enforced by the devicetree.\n\nCan you use `DT_FOREACH_CHILD()` instead?\n\nThe original implementation appears to support 4 colors, but this now only support 2?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 87,
        "startChar": 45,
        "endLine": 87,
        "endChar": 50
      },
      "revId": "82659844b7af5f55f95be5db2ad6cd868fa3ff23",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "ae8d8033_efa0a0f0",
        "filename": "zephyr/shim/src/led_driver/led.c",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 87,
      "author": {
        "id": 1548775
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-07-10T23:12:35Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I can\u0027t use any `FOREACH` macros because this whole macro is called from a `FOREACH`: `static const struct node_prop_t node_array[] \u003d { DT_INST_FOREACH_CHILD(0, SET_LED_VALUES) };` and Zephyr does not support nested `FOREACH` macros.\n\nThe original implementation supports 4 colors shared between all leds, which for all intents and purposes means only 2 maximum leds are supported because for any one led 2 colors are needed to turn it on and off. The current implementation aims to keep the driver logic the same and only change the devicetree so it also only supports 2 leds with 4 colors shared between them. \n\nThe plan for the driver logic overhaul is to expand support to any number of leds and make it not dependent on any node names as each node will be uniquely defined by the `led-node` or `led-color` properties instead.",
      "parentUuid": "f041bdad_dd7ee5b5",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 87,
        "startChar": 45,
        "endLine": 87,
        "endChar": 50
      },
      "revId": "82659844b7af5f55f95be5db2ad6cd868fa3ff23",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "85ea7248_6355a69c",
        "filename": "zephyr/shim/src/led_driver/led.c",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 87,
      "author": {
        "id": 1308749
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-07-11T20:53:52Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "If you move the compatible:\n```\nled-colors {\n  power-state-charge-left {\n    compatible \u003d \"cros-ec,led-policy\";\n    led_0 { ... };\n    led_1 { ... };\n    ...\n    };\n  power-state-charge-right {\n    compatible \u003d \"cros-ec,led-policy\";\n    led_0 { ... };\n    led_1 { ... };\n    ...\n    };\n```\n\nThen you can use `DT_INST_FOREACH_STATUS_OKAY` and nest a call to `DT_INST_FOREACH_CHILD_STATUS_OKAY` to get the LEDs.\n\nExample [here](http://cs/h/chromium/chromiumos/codesearch/+/main:src/third_party/zephyr/main/drivers/input/input_gpio_keys.c?l\u003d165)\n\nYou\u0027ll still need to handle the colors, but this could be organized as an array.\n\n```\n    color \u003d \u003c\"LED_OFF\"1000\u003e, \u003c\"LED_WHITE\" 1000\u003e;\n```\n\nI think this would allow an arbitrary number of LEDs per policy, and an arbitrary number of colors/effects.",
      "parentUuid": "ae8d8033_efa0a0f0",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 87,
        "startChar": 45,
